The bra corresponding to Equation [eq:l4-time-development] is obtained by Hermitian conjugation: $\bra{\psi(t)}=\bra{\psi(0)}U^\dagger(t).$ Substituting the bra and ket evolutions into Equation [eq:l4-overlap-conservation] gives $\bra{\psi(0)}U^\dagger(t)U(t)\ket{\phi(0)}
=
\braket{\psi(0)}{\phi(0)}$ for every pair of initial vectors. If an operator has the same matrix elements as the identity between every pair of vectors, it is the identity. Hence $U^\dagger(t)U(t)=I.$ An operator satisfying Equation [eq:l4-unitarity] is unitary. In the finite-dimensional setting, $U^\dagger(t)=U^{-1}(t),$ so the evolution can be run backward.

